title: 'Update EVM Opcode Benchmarking and Weights'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    Replace JUMPDEST-based estimates for selected EVM opcodes with
    weights derived from their own benchmarks.

    The `evm_opcode` benchmark and WeightInfo method are renamed to
    `evm_jumpdest_opcode`. Rename the method in custom WeightInfo
    implementations and update its callers.

    The following benchmarks and matching WeightInfo methods are added:

    - `evm_jump_opcode`
    - `evm_jumpi_opcode`
    - `evm_pc_opcode`
    - `evm_push_opcode`
    - `evm_dup_opcode`
    - `evm_swap_opcode`
    - `evm_chainid_opcode`
    - `evm_difficulty_opcode`
    - `evm_codesize_opcode`
    - `evm_calldatasize_opcode`
    - `evm_returndatasize_opcode`
    - `evm_calldataload_opcode`
    - `evm_memory_grow`
    - `evm_memory_reallocate`
    - `evm_memory_copy_out`
    - `evm_mload_opcode`
    - `evm_msize_opcode`
    - `evm_mstore_opcode`
    - `evm_mstore8_opcode`
    - `evm_mcopy_opcode`
    - `evm_mcopy_per_byte`
    - `evm_lt_opcode`
    - `evm_gt_opcode`
    - `evm_eq_opcode`
    - `evm_iszero_opcode`
    - `evm_and_opcode`
    - `evm_or_opcode`
    - `evm_xor_opcode`
    - `evm_not_opcode`
    - `evm_byte_opcode`
    - `evm_clz_opcode`
    - `evm_slt_opcode`
    - `evm_sgt_opcode`
    - `evm_shl_opcode`
    - `evm_shr_opcode`
    - `evm_sar_opcode`
    - `evm_add_opcode`
    - `evm_sub_opcode`
    - `evm_mul_opcode`
    - `evm_exp_zero_opcode`
    - `evm_exp_opcode`
    - `evm_exp_per_bit`
    - `evm_signextend_opcode`
    - `evm_dispatch_mix`
    - `evm_div_opcode_three_limb_variant`
    - `evm_sdiv_opcode_three_limb_variant`
    - `evm_mod_opcode_three_limb_variant`
    - `evm_smod_opcode_three_limb_variant`
    - `evm_addmod_opcode_three_limb_variant`

    Below, `slope(f)` is `f(1) - f(0)` for a WeightInfo method `f`. An
    op-code charged from its own benchmark pays the benchmark's slope.
    `JUMP` and `JUMPI` subtract `slope(evm_jumpdest_opcode)`, and the
    op-codes benchmarked in pairs with a `POP` subtract
    `slope(evm_pop_opcode)`. `DIV`, `SDIV`, `MOD`, `SMOD` and `ADDMOD` take
    the largest slope of their benchmark and its `_one_limb_variant`,
    `_three_limb_variant`, `_mixed_variant` and `_fixed_knuth_variant`
    benchmarks before subtracting. Before it runs, every op-code except
    `PUSH`, `DUP` and `SWAP` also pays a dispatch charge, including
    op-codes charged through RuntimeCosts: `slope(evm_dispatch_mix)` minus
    the charges of the 80 op-codes in one block of that benchmark, divided
    by 80.

    These charges depend on the size of an operand:

    - `EXP` with a zero exponent: `slope(evm_exp_zero_opcode)`, plus the
      dispatch charge.
    - `EXP` with an exponent of `L >= 1` bits: `slope(evm_exp_opcode) +
      slope(evm_exp_per_bit) * (L - 1)`, plus the dispatch charge.
    - `MCOPY` of `len` bytes: `slope(evm_mcopy_opcode) +
      slope(evm_mcopy_per_byte) * max(len - 64, 0)`, plus the dispatch
      charge. A zero-length copy pays the fixed part too.
    - Memory growth from `current` to `target` bytes, with `target`
      rounded up to a multiple of 32:
      `evm_memory_grow((target - current) / 32)`. When `target` exceeds
      the capacity of the memory buffer, the capacity becomes `target`
      rounded up to a power of two, and the growth also pays
      `evm_memory_reallocate(capacity / 32)`. The buffer starts at 4 KiB.
      Every op-code whose memory range ends past the current memory size
      pays this, including the input and output ranges of calls.
    - `RETURN` and `REVERT` with `len > 0` bytes of output:
      `evm_memory_copy_out(len)`.

    Regenerate pallet-revive weights for your runtime and implement these
    methods in custom WeightInfo implementations.

    The pallet's `integrity_test` requires the dispatch charge above to be
    greater than zero. If it fails for your runtime, regenerate its
    pallet-revive weights.

    No storage migration is required.
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    Selected EVM op-codes are charged from their own benchmarks, so their
    gas costs change. The exact prices depend on the weights of each
    runtime.

    - Cheaper: `JUMP`, `JUMPI`, `EXP` with small exponents, and long
      `MCOPY` copies.
    - Much more expensive: `DIV`, `SDIV`, `MOD`, `SMOD`, `ADDMOD` and
      `MULMOD`.
    - More expensive: `CALLDATALOAD`, `MLOAD`, `MSTORE`, `EQ`, `MUL`, `EXP`
      with large exponents, and short `MCOPY` copies.

    Existing contracts do not need migration or redeployment. Re-estimate
    gas limits after the runtime upgrade; calls using previous limits
    may run out of gas.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch
